--\n#### 2.3  Constructions for the admissible values  \n\nWe now exhibit explicit configurations for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non\u2011sunny and together they contain every point of\n\\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \nThe only point of \\(S_{n}\\) not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\); it is covered\nby the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ny = x-(n-1) \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThus we have exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3.3  \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Vertical part.**  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{V}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose abscissa does not exceed\n   \\(n-k\\).\n\n2. **Remaining triangular set.**  \n   The points that are still uncovered form  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{T}\n   \\]\n\n   We now construct \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover exactly \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 .\n   \\tag{L}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_i\\) contains the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (i,1),\\;(i+1,2),\\dots ,\\;(k,\\,k-i+1) .\n   \\tag{Li}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) with \\(a\\ge b\\) lies on the unique line\n   \\(L_{a-b+1}\\).\n\n   *Lines of slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{k-1}\\).*  \n   For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k-2\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{k-1}\\,x + \\frac{k+1}{k-1}\\,j .\n   \\tag{M}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) satisfies \\(a<b\\) set \\(j=b-a\\) (note that\n   \\(1\\le j\\le k-2\\)).  Substituting \\((a,b)\\) into (M) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b = -\\frac{1}{k-1}a + \\frac{k+1}{k-1}j\n   \\Longleftrightarrow a+b = k+1-j .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) and \\(j=b-a\\ge 1\\), the equality holds, so the\n   point belongs to the line \\(M_j\\).\n\n   Consequently the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}_k = \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_{k-2}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the set contains exactly \\(k\\) distinct lines; the two families overlap\n   only in the first \\(k-2\\) members of the \\(L_i\\)\u2019s, which we keep once)\n   cover every point of \\(T_k\\) and no point outside \\(T_k\\).\n\n   All slopes involved are \\(1\\) or \\(-\\dfrac{1}{k-1}\\); both differ from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), hence every line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) is sunny.\n\n3. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   The configuration consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (V) together\n   with the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) contains exactly \\(n\\)\n   distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it covers every\n   point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nThus a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nand the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  The constructions above prove\nexistence for the allowed values, and the argument of \u00a72.2 proves the\nexclusion of \\(k=2\\).  Hence the problem is completely solved."
