(a+b=n+1\\), hence belong to\n\\(S_n\\).  \nThe second line has slope \\(-2\\) and passes through \\((m+1,m)\\) and\n\\((m,m+2)\\); again both lie in \\(S_n\\) because\n\\((m+1)+m=n+1-(n-2m-1)\\le n+1\\) and \\(m+(m+2)=2m+2\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus for each even \\(t\\) we obtain **two** distinct sunny lines, each covering\nexactly two points of \\(S_n\\); for each odd \\(t\\) we obtain one sunny line\ncovering at least two points.  Counting the lines:\n\n* if \\(n\\) is odd, we have \\((n-1)/2\\) even indices (giving \\(n-1\\) lines) and\n  \\((n+1)/2\\) odd indices (giving \\((n+1)/2\\) lines).  The total number of\n  distinct lines is  \n\n  \\[\n  (n-1)+(n+1)/2 = n .\n  \\]\n\n* if \\(n\\) is even, we have \\(n/2\\) even indices (giving \\(n\\) lines) and\n  \\(n/2\\) odd indices (giving \\(n/2\\) lines).  Among the \\(n/2\\) odd lines\n  some coincide with lines already produced for even indices; after removing\n  repetitions we are left with exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nAll lines constructed have slopes from the set \\(\\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\), none of\nwhich belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence they are sunny.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Adding a prescribed number of sunny lines\n\nWe now show how to obtain **any** prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n(when \\(n\\ge 4\\)).  The idea is to keep a “frame’’ of vertical non‑sunny lines\nand to cover the remaining right‑hand strip by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 2 (Strip covering)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 4\\) and \\(1\\le t\\le n\\).  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n,t}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid n-t+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(T_{n,t}\\) can be covered by **exactly** \\(t\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(T_{n,t}\\) consists of the points of the triangle \\(S_n\\) whose\n\\(x\\)-coordinate is at least