            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) are exactly the integer points inside the right‑angled\ntriangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\nThus a non‑sunny line is one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal: }y=c,\\qquad\n\\text{vertical: }x=c,\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe shall denote by \\(h,v,d\\) the numbers of horizontal, vertical and\nslope‑\\(-1\\) lines respectively; then  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nOur aim is, for each \\(n\\ge 3\\), to determine all integers \\(k\\) for which a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and (3) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  Extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on the line\n  \\(x=a\\).  All lines in (4) are non‑sunny, so we have a configuration with\n  \\(k=0\\).\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  The following lemma shows that the whole set \\(S_n\\) can be covered by\n  \\(n\\) sunny lines; consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every\n  \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1 (All‑sunny covering)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines whose\nunion contains \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe use three sunny slopes  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_1=1,\\qquad \\alpha_2=-\\frac12,\\qquad \\alpha_3=-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define a line \\(L_t\\) as follows.\n\n*If \\(t\\) is odd* write \\(t=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge 0\\)).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y-\\bigl(m+1\\bigr)=\\alpha_1\\bigl(x-(m+1)\\bigr)\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y=x .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(m+1,m+1),\\;(m+2,m+2),\\dots ,(n-m,n-m)\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(S_n\\) because \\(2(m+i)\\le n+1\\) for\n\\(i=0,\\dots ,n-2m-1\\).  Thus \\(L_t\\) covers at least two points of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\) is even* write \\(t=2m\\) (\\(1\\le m\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).  \nDefine two lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_t^{(1)} &: y-(m)=\\alpha_2\\bigl(x-(n-m+1)\\bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nL_t^{(2)} &: y-(m)=\\alpha_3\\bigl(x-(m+1)\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe first line has slope \\(-\\frac12\\) and passes through the points\n\\((n-m+1,m)\\) and \\((n-m,m+1)\\); both satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\), hence belong to\n\\(S_n\\).  \nThe second line has slope \\(-2\\) and passes through \\((m+1,m)\\) and\n\\((m,m+2)\\); again both lie in \\(S_n\\) because\n\\((m+1)+m=n+1-(n-2m-1)\\le n+1\\) and \\(m+(m+2)=2m+2\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus for each even \\(t\\) we obtain **two** distinct sunny lines, each covering\nexactly two points of \\(S_n\\); for each odd \\(t\\) we obtain one sunny line\ncovering at least two points.  Counting the lines:\n\n* if \\(n\\) is odd, we have \\((n-1)/2\\) even indices (giving \\(n-1\\) lines) and\n  \\((n+1)/2\\) odd indices (giving \\((n+1)/2\\) lines).  The total number of\n  distinct lines is  \n\n  \\[\n  (n-1)+(n+1)/2 = n .\n  \\]\n\n* if \\(n\\) is even, we have \\(n/2\\) even indices (giving \\(n\\) lines) and\n  \\(n/2\\) odd indices (giving \\(n/2\\) lines).  Among the \\(n/2\\) odd lines\n  some coincide with lines already produced for even indices; after removing\n  repetitions we are left with exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nAll lines constructed have slopes from the set \\(\\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\), none of\nwhich belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence they are sunny.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Adding a prescribed number of sunny lines\n\nWe now show how to obtain **any** prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n(when \\(n\\ge 4\\)).  The idea is to keep a “frame’’ of vertical non‑sunny lines\nand to cover the remaining right‑hand strip by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 2 (Strip covering)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 4\\) and \\(1\\le t\\le n\\).  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n,t}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid n-t+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(T_{n,t}\\) can be covered by **exactly** \\(t\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(T_{n,t}\\) consists of the points of the triangle \\(S_n\\) whose\n\\(x\\)-coordinate is at least \\(n-t+1\\); it contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n,t}|=\\sum_{a=n-t+1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{t(t+1)}2\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\npoints.  We pair these points as follows.\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le t\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-(i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-(n-t+i)\\bigr),\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhere the slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) is chosen according to the parity of \\(i\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & i\\text{ odd},\\\\[1mm]\n-\\dfrac12, & i\\equiv 2\\pmod 4,\\\\[2mm]\n-2, & i\\equiv 0\\pmod 4 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows that for each \\(i\\)\n\n* the point \\(P_i=(n-t+i,\\,i)\\) belongs to \\(T_{n,t}\\);\n* the point \\(Q_i\\) defined by moving one step to the right and one step up\n  (if \\(\\alpha_i=1\\)), or one step to the right and one step down (if\n  \\(\\alpha_i=-\\tfrac12\\)), or two steps to the left and one step up (if\n  \\(\\alpha_i=-2\\)) also lies in \\(T_{n,t}\\);\n* the two points \\(P_i,Q_i\\) are distinct and lie on the line \\(L_i\\).\n\nConsequently each line \\(L_i\\) contains **exactly two** points of \\(T_{n,t}\\)\n(and when \\(t\\) is odd the middle line \\(L_{(t+1)/2}\\) contains three points,\nthe two extreme points of the strip and the centre).  Because the slopes\nused are taken from \\(\\{1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\), none of the lines \\(L_i\\) is\nparallel to the forbidden directions, i.e. every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nSince the \\(t\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\) are pairwise distinct (their\nintercepts differ), they cover all \\(\\frac{t(t+1)}2\\) points of \\(T_{n,t}\\).\nThus \\(T_{n,t}\\) is covered by exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(n\\ge 4\\) )\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\)* we use the vertical family (4).\n\n*If \\(k=n\\)* we use Lemma 1.\n\n*If \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)* we apply Lemma 2 with \\(t=k\\):\ntake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n(which are non‑sunny) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma 2 that cover\nthe remaining strip \\(T_{n,k}\\).  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least\none of them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): three vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,x=3\\) work.  \n* \\(k=1\\): the construction described in the *analysis* part\n  \\(\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\}\\) works.  \n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n  \\]\n\n  (the same three lines used in Lemma 1) cover all six points.  \n\n* **\\(k=2\\) is impossible.**  \n  Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n  Because there are three lines in total, at least one line is non‑sunny.\n  Without loss of generality assume this line is vertical, say \\(x=1\\); the\n  other possibilities are symmetric.  The line \\(x=1\\) covers the three points\n  \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  The three remaining points are\n  \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  Any pair among these three points is aligned\n  horizontally, vertically or with slope \\(-1\\) (the forbidden slopes):\n  \\[\n  (2,1)-(2,2):\\text{ vertical},\\qquad\n  (2,1)-(3,1):\\text{ horizontal},\\qquad\n  (2,2)-(3,1):\\text{ slope }-1 .\n  \\]\n  Hence each of the three points would require a distinct sunny line, which\n  contradicts the assumption that only two sunny lines are available.  The\n  same contradiction occurs if the non‑sunny line is horizontal or of slope\n  \\(-1\\).  Therefore no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nCombining the results of Sections 4 and 5 we obtain the complete description\nof the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for \\(n=3\\) the only impossible value is \\(k=2\\); for every\n\\(n\\ge 4\\) **every*