le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose only one line were non‑sunny.  Then all other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny, i.e. none of them is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Any sunny line can contain at most one point of the set \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le n\\}\\) (these points lie on a horizontal line, which is forbidden).  \n   Hence at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would be needed to cover those \\(n-1\\) points, leaving no sunny line to cover any other point – a contradiction.  \n   Consequently at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible because then **all** lines would be sunny, contradicting the previous paragraph.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\text{slope }0\\;( \\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }\\infty\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;( \\text{parallel to }x+y=0).\n\\]\n\nAll other slopes give a sunny line.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Two extreme constructions  \n\n#### Lemma 1 (Covering by the “corner” construction).  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:x=1,\\\\\nL_2&:y=1,\\\\\nL_i&:\\text{the line through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(i,n+1-i),\\qquad 2\\le i\\le n-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThen the family \\(\\mathcal L_n:=\\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly two of which are non‑sunny, and \\(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_n}L\\) contains \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(i\\) the vector \\((i-1,n-i)\\) is primitive (its coordinates are coprime); consequently the line \\(L_i\\) contains precisely the integer points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1+ t(i-1),\\,1+ t(n-i))\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,\\;1+t(i-1)\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points of the segment joining \\((1,1)\\) to \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\) respectively.  \nOtherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\); set  \n\n\\[\nt=\\gcd(a-1,b-1),\\qquad\ni=1+\\frac{a-1}{t},\\qquad\nn-i= \\frac{b-1}{t}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a-1\\) and \\(b-1\\) are positive integers, \\(i\\) is an integer with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)=(1+t(i-1),\\,1+t(n-i))\\) belongs to the segment described in (3), hence lies on \\(L_i\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThe slopes of the lines \\(L_i\\) are \\(\\dfrac{n-i}{i-1}\\).  \nFor \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) this number is never \\(0\\), never \\(\\infty\\) and never \\(-1\\) (the equality \\(\\frac{n-i}{i-1}=-1\\) would give \\(n=1\\), impossible).  \nHence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, while \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are respectively vertical and horizontal, i.e. non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (All‑vertical construction).  \n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncover \\(S_n\\) and are all non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines; from Lemma 2 we obtain a configuration with \\(k=0\\).  \n\n---\n\n### 3.  Interpolating between the two extremes  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i,\\qquad H_i:\\;y=n+1-i\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nthe vertical and horizontal lines that pass through the endpoint \\((i,n+1-i)\\) of the sunny line \\(L_i\\) of Lemma 1.\n\nObserve that  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\subseteq V_i\\cup H_i .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIndeed every point of \\(L_i\\) has abscissa \\(i\\) or ordinate \\(n+1-i\\) (it lies on the segment joining \\((1,1)\\) to \\((i,n+1-i)\\)).  \n\nConsequently, if in the family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) we replace a sunny line \\(L_i\\) by the single non‑sunny line \\(V_i\\) (or \\(H_i\\)), the set of covered points does **not** shrink, while the number of sunny lines decreases by one and the number of non‑sunny lines increases by one.\n\nStarting from the configuration of Lemma 1 (which has exactly two non‑sunny lines), we may perform the replacement described above for any subset of the indices \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n\nIf we replace \\(r\\) of the sunny lines, the resulting family contains  \n\n\\[\n2+r\\quad\\text{non‑sunny lines},\\qquad (n-2)-r\\quad\\text{sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0\\le r\\le n-2\\), the number of sunny lines that can be obtained is precisely every integer  \n\n\\[\nk=(n-2)-r\\quad\\text{with }0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines remain distinct because the vertical (or horizontal) lines introduced have abscissae (or ordinates) different from those of the already present lines.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nThen at most one line is non‑sunny.  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nH:=\\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset S_n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(H\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  \nHence no sunny line can contain two distinct points of \\(H\\); each sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(H\\).  \n\nIf there is exactly one non‑sunny line, it can cover at most all points of a single vertical or horizontal line, i.e. at most one point of \\(H\\).  \nTherefore at least \\(|H|=n-1\\) sunny lines would be required to cover the remaining points of \\(H\\).  \nBut we have only \\(k\\le n-1\\) sunny lines, leaving no sunny line for any other point of \\(S_n\\), a contradiction.  \n\nHence a configuration must contain **at least two** non‑sunny lines, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k=n\\) is ruled out by (7) as well.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the existence result of Sections 2–3 with the impossibility proved in Section 4, we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are exactly }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
