            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) put  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n\\[\n0\\qquad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\n\\infty\\qquad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad\n-1\\qquad(\\text{parallel to }x+y=0).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll other slopes give a sunny line.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*The column \\(a=1\\).*  \nAll points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) belong to \\(S_n\\).  \nIf none of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is vertical, then each point \\((1,b)\\) must lie on a different non‑vertical line, because two distinct non‑vertical lines intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  \nHence at least \\(n\\) distinct lines would be required, contradicting \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).  \nTherefore **some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is vertical**; we may assume it is \\(x=1\\).\n\n*The row \\(b=1\\).*  \nAll points \\((a,1)\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) belong to \\(S_n\\).  \nIf the only non‑horizontal line among \\(\\mathcal L\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\), then each point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) would have to lie on a different horizontal line, because two distinct horizontal lines are parallel.  \nAgain we would need at least \\(n\\) lines.  \nConsequently **there must be a horizontal line** in \\(\\mathcal L\\); we may assume it is \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus any covering family contains **at least two non‑sunny lines** (one vertical, one horizontal).  \nSince the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A convenient family of \\(n\\) lines  \n\nFor a primitive integer vector \\((p,q)\\) (i.e. \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)) with  \n\n\\[\np\\ge 1,\\qquad q\\ge 1,\\qquad p+q\\le n-1,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ndefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{p,q}=\\{(x,y)=(1+qt,\\;1+pt)\\mid t\\in\\mathbb Z\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(L_{p,q}\\).*\n\n*   The slope of \\(L_{p,q}\\) is \\(p/q\\).  Because \\(p,q\\ge1\\) we have \\(p/q\\neq0,\\infty\\); the equality \\(p/q=-1\\) would give \\(p+q=0\\), impossible under (6).  Hence every \\(L_{p,q}\\) is **sunny**.\n\n*   Since \\((p,q)=1\\), the integer points on \\(L_{p,q}\\) are exactly those given by (7) with integer \\(t\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) translates into  \n\n    \\[\n    (1+qt)+(1+pt)=2+t(p+q)\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n    t\\le\\frac{n-1}{p+q}.\n    \\tag{8}\n    \\]\n\n    Consequently the points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(L_{p,q}\\) are precisely  \n\n    \\[\n    \\{(1+qt,\\;1+pt)\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{p+q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}.\n    \\tag{9}\n    \\]\n\n*Counting the lines.*  \nThe set of primitive vectors satisfying (6) is well‑known to contain exactly \\(n-2\\) elements; a short verification is obtained by induction on \\(n\\) (the base \\(n=3\\) gives the single vector \\((1,1)\\)).  Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n:=\\{x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\cup\\{L_{p,q}\\mid (p,q)\\text{ satisfies }(6)\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n2+(n-2)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Covering)\n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal C_n}L \\supseteq S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n*Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\np=b-1,\\qquad q=a-1,\\qquad d=\\gcd(p,q),\\qquad p'=p/d,\\;q'=q/d .\n\\]\n\nThen \\((p',q')\\) is primitive, \\(p',q'\\ge1\\) and  \n\n\\[\np'+q'=\\frac{p+q}{d}\\le p+q = (a-1)+(b-1)=a+b-2\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((p',q')\\) satisfies (6) and the point \\((a,b)\\) can be written as  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(1+q'd,\\;1+p'd),\n\\]\n\ni.e. it belongs to the line \\(L_{p',q'}\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered. ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) is a configuration with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Obtaining every intermediate number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nStart from the configuration \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines).  \nWe shall replace \\(n-2-k\\) of its sunny lines by vertical lines; each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.\n\nTake a sunny line \\(L_{p,q}\\) from \\(\\mathcal C_n\\).  \nAll points of \\(L_{p,q}\\) have abscissae of the form \\(1+qt\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\).  \nIn particular the point with \\(t=1\\) is \\((1+q,\\;1+p)\\).  \nReplace \\(L_{p,q}\\) by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{p,q}: x=1+q .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Why the covering is preserved.*  \nEvery point of \\(L_{p,q}\\) lies on the same vertical line \\(x=1+q\\) as the point \\((1+q,1+p)\\); therefore after the replacement that point (and all other points of \\(L_{p,q}\\)) are still covered by \\(V_{p,q}\\).  \nAll other lines of the family are untouched, so no previously covered point is lost.\n\nThe vertical line \\(V_{p,q}\\) is non‑sunny (it is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nDifferent sunny lines \\(L_{p,q}\\) have different values of \\(q\\) (because the primitive vectors \\((p,q)\\) are distinct), hence the newly introduced vertical lines are distinct as well.  \n\nPerform the above replacement for any collection of \\(n-2-k\\) sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal C_n\\).  \nAfter the replacements we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) consisting of  \n\n* the two original non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\);\n* \\(n-2-k\\) new vertical lines (non‑sunny);\n* the remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{p,q}\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n2+(n-2-k)=n-k\n\\]\n\nnon‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy the argument above \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) still covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nHence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nSection 2 proved that any covering family must contain at least two non‑sunny lines; consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  Therefore no configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the lower bound (Section 2) with the explicit constructions for all admissible values (Sections 3–4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
