\).  \n   If \\(L_i\\) is kept, the point is still covered.  \n   If \\(L_i\\) is replaced by a vertical line, then the abscissa of the point is \\(a\\).  \n   Because the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) contains **all** possible abscissae that can appear in a point of a strip whose sunny line has been removed (see Lemma\u202f2 below), the point is covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n   Hence after the replacement of any collection of \\(n-k\\) sunny lines the resulting family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\\bigl(\\{L_i\\mid L_i\\text{ kept}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{V_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   still covers \\(S_n\\).  By construction \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny (vertical) lines, i.e. it satisfies the required conditions.\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f2 (vertical\u2011line sufficiency).**  \n   Let \\(I\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) be the set of indices of the sunny lines that are *removed*.  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{i\\in I}L_i\\subseteq\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n-|I|}\\{(j,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;j+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   For a fixed \\(i\\in I\\) the strip \\(D_i\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\le b-a\\le i+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\ge b-i-1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Because every point of \\(S_n\\) also satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le n-i .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the abscissa \\(a\\) of any point of \\(L_i\\) lies in the interval \\([1,n-i]\\).  \n   Since \\(|I|=n-k\\), the largest possible value of \\(i\\) among the removed lines is at most \\(n-k-1\\); consequently \\(n-i\\ge k+1\\).  \n   Therefore every such abscissa belongs to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), i.e. to one of the vertical lines \\(V_j\\) introduced in (5). \u220e\n\n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that the vertical lines introduced in (5) indeed cover all points that might have been lost when the corresponding sunny lines are removed.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   We have exhibited configurations for the two extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) and shown how to obtain a configuration for any intermediate integer \\(k\\) by a controlled replacement of sunny lines with vertical lines, using Lemma\u202f2 to guarantee that no point of \\(S_n\\) becomes uncovered.  Hence every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   can be realised.  No larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non\u2011negative integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]"

