\\(\\frac{i+1}{i}=1+\\frac1i>1\\); the line is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\), so it is indeed *sunny*. \\(L_0\\) (the line \\(y=x\\)) also has slope \\(1\\) and is sunny. Thus each individual line satisfies the “sunny” condition.\n\n**Critical Error 1 – Incomplete partition of \\(S_n\\).**  \nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains points with negative differences \\(b-a\\). For example, with \\(n=4\\) the point \\((3,1)\\) belongs to \\(S_4\\) (since \\(3+1=4\\le5\\)) but has \\(b-a=-2\\). The definition of the strips \\(D_i\\) only allows \\(i\\ge0\\); therefore \\((3,1)\\) is not in any \\(D_i\\). Consequently the claim that the \\(D_i\\) “partition” \\(S_n\\) is false. The partition omits roughly half of the points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n**Critical Error 2 – Incorrect claim that points of \\(D_i\\) lie on \\(L_i\\).**  \nEven restricting attention to a single strip, the asserted incidence is wrong. Take \\(i=1\\). Then \\(L_1\\) is the line \\(y=2x-2\\). A generic point of \\(D_1\\) satisfies \\(b-a=1\\) (or \\(2\\)), i.e. \\((a,a+1)\\) or \\((a,a+2)\\). Substituting \\((a,a+1)\\) into \\(y=2x-2\\) yields \\(a+1=2a-2\\), i.e. \\(a=3\\). Only the specific point \\((3,4)\\) (if it belongs to \\(S_n\\)) lies on \\(L_1\\); all other points of the strip do **not**. Hence the statement “every point of \\(D_i\\) lies on \\(L_i\\)” is false.\n\nBecause the two statements above are false, the derived equality (4) \\(S_n=\\bigcup_{i=0}^{n-1}L_i\\) is also false. Consequently the claimed “all‑sunny” configuration does **not** exist.\n\n**Result:** The construction for \\(k=n\\) is invalid; the solution provides no correct example for the case \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4. From the extremes to every intermediate value\n\n> **“Start from the all‑sunny configuration \\(\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\\).  \n> For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we replace \\(n-k\\) of the sunny lines by the vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k) .\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]”**  \n\n> **“… the resulting family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) … still covers \\(S_n\\).”**\n\n**Verification:** This step **depends critically** on the existence of a valid all‑sunny configuration. Since the all‑sunny configuration is not established (see Critical Errors 1–2), the argument that “replacing any \\(n-k\\) sunny lines by vertical lines preserves coverage” has no foundation. Even if a correct all‑sunny configuration existed, the proof that the replacement preserves coverage would still require a rigorous justification, which is lacking (see Lemma 2 below). Therefore this entire part of the solution is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Lemma 2 (vertical‑line sufficiency)\n\n> **“Lemma 2 (vertical‑line sufficiency).  \n> Let \\(I\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) be the set of indices of the sunny lines that are *removed*.  \n> Then  \n> \\[\n> \\bigcup_{i\\in I}L_i\\subseteq\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n-|I|}\\{(j,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;j+b\\le n+1\\}.\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]”**  \n\n> **“Proof. … Since \\(|I|=n-k\\), the largest possible value of \\(i\\) among the removed lines is at most \\(n-k-1\\); consequently \\(n-i\\ge k+1\\). … Therefore every such abscissa belongs to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).”**\n\n**Verification – Critical Error 3 (incorrect bound on removed indices).**  \nThe set \\(I\\) can be any subset of \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(n-k\\). There is **no guarantee** that the *largest* index in \\(I\\) is \\(\\le n-k-1\\). For instance, with \\(n=5\\) and \\(k=2\\) we have \\(|I|=3\\); we could choose \\(I=\\{2,3,4\\}\\). Here the largest removed index is \\(4\\), which is larger than \\(n-k-1=2\\). The inequality \\(n-i\\ge k+1\\) fails for \\(i=4\\) (since \\(n-i=1<k+1=3\\)). Hence the argument that the abscissa of any point on a removed line lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) is false.\n\nBecause the proof of Lemma 2 collapses, the claim that the vertical lines \\(V_j\\) cover all points lost when sunny lines are removed is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Conclusion\n\n> **“Hence every integer \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can be realised. No larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.”**\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe statement “no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible” is trivially true (you cannot have more sunny lines than total lines). However, the claim that **every** integer \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable rests on the flawed constructions for \\(k=n\\) and the invalid Lemma 2. Since the existence of a configuration for \\(k=n\\) has not been demonstrated, the conclusion is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\n- The **all‑non‑sunny** construction (k=0) is correct.\n- The **all‑sunny** construction (k=n) is **incorrect**; the proposed lines do not cover the required set of points.\n- The **intermediate‑k** constructions rely on the invalid all‑sunny configuration and on a flawed Lemma 2; consequently they are not justified.\n- Multiple steps contain **Critical Errors** that break the logical chain of the proof.\n\n**Therefore the solution is invalid.**"
