-\n\n### 3. Lemma 1 – “any sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_m\\)”\n\n> “*Lemma 1.* In the set \\(T_m\\) (\\(m\\ge 2\\)) any sunny line contains **at most two** points of \\(T_m\\).  \n> *Proof.* Suppose a sunny line contains three distinct points of \\(T_m\\).  \n> After ordering them by increasing \\(x\\)–coordinate, the differences between consecutive points are equal to a non‑zero integer vector \\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=d\\ge 2\\) (otherwise the three points would be collinear with a step of length 1 and the line would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions).  \n> Hence both \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are even.  \n> Moving two steps along the line increases the sum \\(x+y\\) by \\(2(p+q)\\ge 8\\).  \n> Because every point of \\(T_m\\) satisfies \\(x+y \\le m+1\\), the smallest possible value of \\(x+y\\) for a point of \\(T_m\\) is \\(2\\); after two steps the sum would be at least \\(10\\), which exceeds \\(m+1\\) for every \\(m\\ge 2\\).  \n> This contradiction shows that three points cannot lie on a sunny line, proving the lemma. ∎”\n\n**Verification:**  \n\n*Counterexample to the statement.*  \nTake \\(m=5\\). The points \\((1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\) all belong to \\(T_5\\) (since \\(1+1=2\\le6\\), \\(2+2=4\\le6\\), \\(3+3=6\\le6\\)). They lie on the line \\(y=x\\), whose slope is \\(1\\); this line is **sunny** (it is not horizontal, vertical, nor slope \\(-1\\)). Hence a sunny line can contain **three** points of \\(T_m\\).  \n\n*Flaw in the proof.*  \nThe argument assumes that the direction vector \\((p,q)\\) between consecutive lattice points must satisfy \\(\\gcd(p,q)=d\\ge2\\). In fact, the primitive direction vector of a line through lattice points can have \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) (e.g. \\((1,1)\\) for the line \\(y=x\\)). The claim “otherwise the three points would be collinear with a step of length 1 and the line would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions” is false; a step of length 1 with direction \\((1,1)\\) yields slope \\(1\\), which is sunny.  \n\nConsequently the lemma is **false**, and the proof contains an unjustified (and incorrect) claim about parity of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\).  \n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – the lemma is false; the proof is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Deriving the bound \\(k\\le3\\)\n\n> “Consequently, if exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, they can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n> The whole set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \n> The \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; each of them can contain at most \\(n\\) points, so together they cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  \n> Hence the sunny lines must cover at least  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-k)n=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n> \\]  \n> points.  \n> By Lemma 1 the sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points, therefore  \n> \\[\n> 2k\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k(k+1)\\le4k\\Longrightarrow k\\le3 .\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \n\n1. **Use of Lemma 1.**  \n   The statement “sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points” directly relies on Lemma 1, which we have shown to be false. Hence this bound is unjustified.\n\n2. **Algebraic error.**  \n   The lower bound for points that must be covered by sunny lines is computed as  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-k)n.\n   \\]  \n   Expanding correctly:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n^2+n}{2} - (n^2 - kn) = \\frac{n^2+n -2n^2 +2kn}{2}\n   = \\frac{-n^2 + n + 2kn}{2}\n   = \\frac{n(2k - n + 1)}{2}.\n   \\]  \n   The solution incorrectly simplifies this expression to \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\). The equality would hold only when \\(n=k\\), which is not assumed. Therefore the derived inequality \\(2k \\ge \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) is based on a **miscalculation**.\n\n3. **Consequences.**  \n   Because both the upper bound on sunny‑line coverage (via Lemma 1) and the lower bound on required sunny‑line coverage are incorrect, the conclusion \\(k\\le3\\) is **unsupported**.\n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – algebraic mistake and reliance on a false lemma invalidate the bound \\(k\\le3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Excluding \\(k=2\\)\n\n> “Assume that exactly two sunny lines are used.  \n> Use \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-2\\); they cover all points with \\(a\\le n-2\\).  \n> The points that remain uncovered are \\((n-1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(n-1,2)\\).  \n> Any line through two of these three points is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); all three directions are forbidden.  \n> Hence no sunny line can contain two of the three points, so at least three sunny lines are necessary – a contradiction.  \n> Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe uncovered points are correctly identified. The slopes of the three possible connecting lines are indeed \\(0\\) (horizontal), undefined (vertical), and \\(-1\\) respectively. Since sunny lines cannot be parallel to any of these three directions, a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points. With only two sunny lines, at least one point would remain uncovered. The argument is sound.  \n\n**Correct.**\n\n---\n\n### 6. Conclusion\n\n> “From steps 4 and 5 we have proved that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n> Step 2 shows that each of these three values can indeed be realised for every \\(n\\ge3\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \nBecause **Step 4** is invalid (critical errors in Lemma 1 and the algebra), the deduction that “no \\(k>3\\) is possible” is not established. Consequently the conclusion that the admissible set of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) is **unsupported** by the presented argument.  \n\n**Result:** The final claim is not justified.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\n- The constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) are correct, and the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) is proved rigorously.  \n- However, the proof that **no larger \\(k\\) can occur** relies on a false lemma and a miscalculated inequality. These are **critical errors** that break the logical chain needed to exclude \\(k\\ge4\\).  \n\nTherefore the solution, as written, is **incorrect**. It does not provide a valid proof for the full classification of admissible \\(k\\)."
