            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{m}|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector \\((p,q)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\np\\neq 0,\\qquad q\\neq 0,\\qquad p+q\\neq 0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(q=0\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(p=0\\)), nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(p+q=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Inductive covering of a triangular lattice)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_{m}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe argue by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base cases.*  \n\n* \\(m=0\\): the empty family of lines covers the empty set.  \n* \\(m=1\\): \\(T_{1}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and contains the point.  \n* \\(m=2\\): \\(T_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). The two sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{1}:\\;y=x,\\qquad L_{2}:\\;y=2x-1\n  \\]\n\n  cover the three points, and they are distinct and sunny.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(m\\le 2\\).\n\n*Induction step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(m-1\\ge 2\\); we construct a family for \\(m\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there are \\(m-1\\) distinct sunny lines\n\\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\}\\) covering all points of \\(T_{m-1}\\).\n\nThe set \\(T_{m}\\) consists of \\(T_{m-1}\\) together with the single new point  \n\n\\[\nP:=(m,1) .\n\\]\n\nWe shall add one more sunny line that passes through \\(P\\) and does **not**\ncoincide with any line already in \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}\\;:\\;\\text{through }P=(m,1)\\text{ and }Q_{t}=(1,t).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\ns(t)=\\frac{t-1}{1-m}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\ge 3\\), the denominator \\(1-m\\) is non‑zero; hence \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is never vertical.\nMoreover \\(s(t)=0\\) would require \\(t=1\\), which we avoid by taking \\(t\\ge 2\\);  \n\\(s(t)=-1\\) would require \\(t-1=-(1-m)\\), i.e. \\(t=m\\), which we also avoid by taking \\(t\\le m-1\\).  \nThus for any \\(t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) the line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is sunny.\n\nThe set \\(\\{s(t)\\mid 2\\le t\\le m-1\\}\\) contains \\(m-2\\) distinct real numbers, while the family \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\) contains only \\(m-1\\) distinct slopes (each line has a single slope). Consequently we can choose a value \\(t_{0}\\) in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) such that \\(s(t_{0})\\) is different from the slope of every line in \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\). (If \\(m=3\\) the set \\(\\{2,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) is \\(\\{2\\}\\); the slope \\(s(2)=\\frac{1}{-2}=-\\frac12\\) is not equal to the slopes of the two lines already constructed for \\(T_{2}\\), so the choice is still possible.)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{m}:=\\ell_{t_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(L_{m}\\) is sunny, distinct from all lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\), and it contains the new point \\(P\\).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m}:=\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\cup\\{L_{m}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_{m}\\) (the points of \\(T_{m-1}\\) are already covered, and \\(P\\) is covered by \\(L_{m}\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Extension to the original triangle)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply Lemma 1 with \\(m=k\\) to obtain a family \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\nNow consider the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each of them contains all points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\).\n\nObserve that the translation  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)\\longmapsto (a-(n-k),\\,b)\n\\]\n\nmaps the subset  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbijectively onto \\(T_{k}\\). Consequently every point of \\(T_{n}\\) with first coordinate at least \\(n-k+1\\) lies on one of the sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) (after undoing the translation).  \n\nThus the union  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{V_{1},\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal S_{k}\n\\]\n\ncontains **all** points of \\(T_{n}\\); it consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) Lemma 2 furnishes a concrete configuration satisfying the two requirements of the problem statement. Hence every such \\(k\\) is admissible.\n\nConversely, a configuration with \\(n\\) lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, because the total number of lines is \\(n\\). Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}