ith \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only value of \\(k\\) that cannot be realised is \\(k=2\\); all other non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) are attainable.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{m}|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nLet the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines be non‑sunny; each of them is either vertical,\nhorizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n* A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n* A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) has the form \\(x+y=d\\) and contains exactly\n  \\(\\min\\{d-1,\\,2n+2-d\\}\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nIn every case the number of points covered by a non‑sunny line is at most \\(n\\).\nHence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny\nlines is at most \\((n-2)n\\).\n\nThe two sunny lines together contain at most \\(2n\\) points (a line can meet a\ncolumn in at most one point).  Consequently the whole configuration can cover\nat most  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)n+2n=n^{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nwhich is strictly smaller than \\(|T_{n}|\\).  Therefore a configuration with\nexactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  One sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered.\nThe only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); it lies on the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\;y = x-(n-1) .\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering a whole triangle with sunny lines\n\n#### Lemma 3.1  \nFor every integer \\(t\\ge 3\\) the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{t}= \\Bigl\\{\\,L_{i}\\;:\\;L_{i}\\colon y = x + (t+1-2i),\\; i=1,\\dots ,t \\Bigr\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_{t}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line \\(L_{i}\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny) and passes through the\npoint \\((i,\\,t+1-i)\\) on the hypotenuse \\(a+b=t+1\\).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{t}\\) we have \\(b-a\\in\\{t-1,t-3,\\dots ,-(t-1)\\}\\);\nthere is a unique index \\(i\\) with \\(t+1-2i = b-a\\), i.e. \\(i=\\frac{t+1-(b-a)}2\\).\nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{i}\\) and belongs to\n\\(L_{i}\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3.2  \nFor every integer \\(t\\ge 4\\) the set \\(T_{t}\\) can be covered by exactly\n\\(t-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nRemove from \\(\\mathcal S_{t}\\) the line \\(L_{1}\\) that contains the point\n\\((1,1)\\).  The remaining \\(t-1\\) lines cover all points of \\(T_{t}\\) except\n\\((1,1)\\) and \\((t,1)\\).  The line  \n\n\\[\nL^{*}\\colon y = \\frac{1}{t-1}\\,x + \\frac{t-2}{t-1}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(\\frac{1}{t-1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) and passes through both\n\\((1,1)\\) and \\((t,1)\\).  Adding \\(L^{*}\\) to the \\(t-1\\) remaining lines\ngives a family of exactly \\(t-1\\) sunny lines covering the whole triangle\n\\(T_{t}\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny\n  line \\(y=x-(n-1)\\) (Section 2).\n\n* **\\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n  Write