            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny lines\n\nA non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nIn a fixed column \\(x=a\\) each of these three types meets the column in at most\none lattice point; consequently every non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\)\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_{n}\\)\nand that exactly two of them are sunny.  Let  \n\n\\[\nv:=|\\{\\,L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is vertical}\\}|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of vertical lines.  The remaining \\(n-v\\) lines are non‑vertical;\namong them precisely two are sunny, the others are horizontal or diagonal.\n\nConsider a column \\(x=a\\) that is **not** covered by a vertical line.\nThe column contains  \n\n\\[\nh_{a}=n+1-a\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\); one of them lies on the hypotenuse\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) and the other \\(h_{a}-1=n-a\\) points are interior.\n\nEach non‑vertical line can meet the column in at most one lattice point,\nhence the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contribute at most \\(n-v\\) points\nto that column.  Therefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-a\\le n-v\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad a\\ge v .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus every column with index smaller than \\(v\\) must be vertical.\nConsequently the set of vertical columns is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,v\\}\\).\n\nNow look at the two right‑most columns, namely \\(a=n-1\\) and \\(a=n\\).\nBoth satisfy \\(a\\ge v\\); hence they are **not** vertical.\nEach of these columns contains two points of \\(T_{n}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na=n-1 &: \\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\\\\na=n   &: \\;(n,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line meets a column in exactly one lattice point, and a horizontal\nor diagonal line also meets a column in at most one point.  Because we have\nonly two sunny lines, at most two of the three points listed above can be\ncovered by sunny lines; the remaining point would have to be covered by a\nhorizontal or diagonal line, but such a line would be parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore could meet at\nmost one of the three columns, leaving at least one point uncovered.\nHence a covering with exactly two sunny lines is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### 3.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on the line with its first coordinate, and no\nline is sunny.\n\n#### 3.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\;y=x-(n-1) .\n\\]\n\nAll points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical\nlines; the only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(L_{1}\\).\n\n#### 3.3  A family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the triangle \\(T_{k}\\)\n(\\(k\\ge 3\\))\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every integer \\(k\\ge 3\\) there\nexist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n*Base \\(k=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y = -2x+5      &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\\\\\nL_{2}&: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\nL_{3}&: y =  x          &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(-2,-\\tfrac12,1\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and together\ncontain the six points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the claim true for some \\(k\\ge 3\\); let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{k}=\\{L^{(k)}_{1},\\dots ,L^{(k)}_{k}\\}\n\\]\n\nbe a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).\nConsider \\(T_{k+1}\\).  Its hypotenuse consists of the points  \n\n\\[\nH_{k+1}=\\{(i,k+2-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(H_{k+1}\\) except \\((1,k+1)\\) already belong to the\nhypotenuse of \\(T_{k}\\) and are therefore covered by \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\).\nReplace the line \\(L^{(k)}_{1}\\) by a new line \\(L^{(k+1)}_{1}\\) that has\nthe **same slope** as \\(L^{(k)}_{1}\\) but passes through the point\n\\((1,k+1)\\).  Because the slope is unchanged, \\(L^{(k+1)}_{1}\\) meets every\ncolumn of \\(T_{k}\\) in exactly the same lattice point as \\(L^{(k)}_{1}\\);\nhence all points of \\(T_{k}\\) remain covered.  The new line now also covers\n\\((1,k+1)\\).  Keeping the remaining \\(k-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) we\nobtain a family of \\(k+1\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+1}\\).\n\nThus, by induction, for every \\(k\\ge 3\\) there exists a set of exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines whose union contains \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n#### 3.4  \\(k\\ge 3\\) for the original problem\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nCover the leftmost \\(n-k\\) columns by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAfter deleting these columns the remaining points are precisely \\(T_{k}\\).\nBy the result of §3.3 we can cover \\(T_{k}\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nTogether with the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines of (3) we obtain a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n*   For \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) explicit constructions are given.\n*   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a construction is obtained\n    by combining vertical lines with the inductively built sunny lines.\n*   It is impossible to have exactly two sunny lines (Section 2).\n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required\nconfiguration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
