cal}\\}|\\) be the number of vertical lines.  \n> The remaining \\(n-v\\) lines are non‑vertical; among them precisely two are sunny, the others are horizontal or diagonal.”\n\n*Correct.*  The notation and counting of vertical versus non‑vertical lines is accurate.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Column‑count inequality  \n\n> “Consider a column \\(x=a\\) that is **not** covered by a vertical line.  \n> The column contains \\(h_{a}=n+1-a\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); one of them lies on the hypotenuse \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the other \\(h_{a}-1=n-a\\) points are interior.  \n> Each non‑vertical line can meet the column in at most one lattice point, hence the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contribute at most \\(n-v\\) points to that column.  Therefore we must have  \n> \\[\n> n-a\\le n-v\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad a\\ge v .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]”\n\n*Justification Gap.*  The column actually contains **\\(h_{a}=n+1-a\\)** points, not just the interior \\(n-a\\) points.  All points of the column must be covered, including the hypotenuse point, because the column is assumed non‑vertical.  The correct inequality should be \\(n+1-a\\le n-v\\), i.e. \\(a\\ge v+1\\).  The argument nevertheless yields the same qualitative conclusion (columns \\(1,\\dots ,v\\) must be vertical), but the omission of the hypotenuse point is an unjustified step.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Identification of the vertical columns  \n\n> “Thus every column with index smaller than \\(v\\) must be vertical.  \n> Consequently the set of vertical columns is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,v\\}\\).”\n\n*Correct (given the previous conclusion).*  Even though the previous inequality was slightly off, the derived statement that columns \\(1\\) through \\(v\\) are vertical remains valid.\n\n---\n\n### 6. The two right‑most columns  \n\n> “Now look at the two right‑most columns, namely \\(a=n-1\\) and \\(a=n\\).  \n> Both satisfy \\(a\\ge v\\); hence they are **not** vertical.  \n> Each of these columns contains two points of \\(T_{n}\\):  \n> \\[\n> a