=n-1\\) and \\(a=n\\).  \n> Both satisfy \\(a\\ge v\\); hence they are **not** vertical.  \n> Each of these columns contains two points of \\(T_{n}\\):  \n> \\[\n> a=n-1 :\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\qquad\n> a=n   :\\;(n,1).\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct.*  The description of the points in the two right‑most columns is accurate.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Claim about sunny lines  \n\n> “A sunny line meets a column in exactly one lattice point, and a horizontal or diagonal line also meets a column in at most one point.”\n\n*Justification Gap.*  A sunny (non‑vertical) line may **miss** a column entirely; it is not guaranteed to meet the column at all.  The statement “exactly one” is therefore unjustified.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Counting the points covered by sunny lines  \n\n> “Because we have only two sunny lines, at most two of the three points listed above can be covered by sunny lines; the remaining point would have to be covered by a horizontal or diagonal line, but such a line would be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore could meet at most one of the three columns, leaving at least one point uncovered.”\n\n*Critical Error.*  The assertion that a horizontal line (or a line of slope \\(-1\\)) can meet **at most one** of the columns \\(\\{x=n-1,\\,x=n\\}\\) is false.  \n- The horizontal line \\(y=1\\) passes through both \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n- The diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) passes through both \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n\nThus a non‑sunny line can cover **two** of the three points, and the remaining point can be covered by the second sunny line.  Consequently the argument that “exactly two sunny lines is impossible” collapses.  This is a decisive logical flaw that invalidates Section 2.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Construction for \\(k=0\\)  \n\n> “Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n> Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on the line with its first coordinate, and no line is sunny.”\n\n*Correct.*  This gives a valid configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 10. Construction for \\(k=1\\)  \n\n> “Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the sunny line \\(L_{1}: y=x-(n-1)\\).  \n> All points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical lines; the only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(L_{1}\\).”\n\n*Correct.*  The line \\(y=x-(n-1)\\) has slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and indeed passes through \\((n,1)\\).  All points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Inductive construction for \\(k\\ge3\\) – base case  \n\n> “Base \\(k=3\\).  \n> The three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> L_{1}: y=-2x+5\\;(1,3),(2,1),\\qquad\n> L_{2}: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\;(1,2),(3,1),\\qquad\n> L_{3}: y=x\\;(1,1),(2,2)\n> \\]  \n> have slopes \\(-2,-\\tfrac12,1\\) and together contain the six points of \\(T_{3}\\).”\n\n*Correct.*  The three lines are indeed sunny and together cover all six points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 12. Inductive step – claim of preserving coverage  \n\n> “Assume the claim true for some \\(k\\ge3\\); let \\(\\mathcal S_{k}=\\{L^{(k)}_{1},\\dots ,L^{(k)}_{k}\\}\\) be a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).  \n> Consider \\(T_{k+1}\\). Its hypotenuse consists of the points \\(H_{k+1}=\\{(i,k+2-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).  \n> All points of \\(H_{k+1}\\) except \\((1,k+1)\\) already belong to the hypotenuse of \\(T_{k}\\) and are therefore covered by \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\).  \n> Replace the line \\(L^{(k)}_{1}\\) by a new line \\(L^{(k+1)}_{1}\\) that has the **same slope** as \\(L^{(k)}_{1}\\) but passes through the point \\((1,k+1)\\).  Because the slope is unchanged, \\(L^{(k+1)}_{1}\\) meets every column of \\(T_{k}\\) in exactly the same lattice point as \\(L^{(k)}_{1}\\); hence all points of \\(T_{k}\\) remain covered.  The new line now also covers \\((1,k+1)\\).  Keeping the remaining \\(k-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) we obtain a family of \\(k+1\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+1}\\).”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \n- A line parallel to \\(L^{(k)}_{1}\\) that passes through a different point **does not** intersect each column at the same lattice point.  If the original line meets column \\(a\\) at \\((a,b_{a})\\), the new parallel line meets the same column at \\((a,b_{a}+\\Delta)\\) where \\(\\Delta\\) is the vertical shift needed to pass through \\((1,k+1)\\).  In general \\(b_{a}+\\Delta\\) will **not** belong to \\(T_{k}\\) (it may exceed the bound \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) or may not be an integer).  Hence the claim that “all points of \\(T_{k}\\) remain covered” is false.  The inductive step collapses, and the existence of a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\) for all \\(k\\ge3\\) remains unproved.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Combining with vertical lines for the original problem  \n\n> “Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n> Cover the leftmost \\(n-k\\) columns by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n> After deleting these columns the remaining points are precisely \\(T_{k}\\).  \n> By the result of §3.3 we can cover \\(T_{k}\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n> Together with the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.”\n\n*Critical Error (consequence).*  This construction depends on the (incorrect) result of §3.3.  Since the existence of a sunny‑only covering of \\(T_{k}\\) for arbitrary \\(k\\ge3\\) has not been established, the whole family for \\(k\\ge3\\) is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Final conclusion  \n\n> “Hence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\).”\n\n*Invalid.*  Because the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) and the existence of configurations for all \\(k\\ge3\\) have not been correctly proved, the conclusion is not justified.\n\n---\n\n**Overall assessment:** The solution contains multiple **Critical Errors** (Sections 2 and 3.3) that break the logical chain, as well as a few **Justification Gaps** (minor omissions in Section 2).  Consequently the answer is not established, and the solution must be regarded as incorrect."
